.TH CCHAN_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cchan_create \- create a two-way communication cchan
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

cchan_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a two-way cchan. The channel is made of two circular
queues that carry messages between an owner thread and the worker
threads, one queue per direction, and both have the same capacity.
Memory comes from the standard malloc and free.

It is the same as calling cchan_create_mp() with NULL memory
management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size   Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold.
           Because one circular queue holds each direction, the same
           SIZE_MAX / sizeof(cmessage) bound that
           ccircq_create(3) documents applies here.
err_str    Optional pointer for an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new cchan, or NULL on failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe. When you are done with the channel,
destroy it with cchan_destroy().

Each call picks its direction from the ID of the calling thread. The
owner is the thread that called cchan_create(), and it must stay
alive for as long as the cchan is in use: The channel routes a
message by comparing the caller's thread ID with the one it took at
creation. Once the owner exits, the OS can hand the same ID to a new,
unrelated thread, and the channel would then treat that thread as the
owner too without reporting anything.

fork(2) and a cchan can be combined in two supported ways: Call
fork(2) before you create the cchan, or create it and then call
fork(2) followed at once by exec(3). A program that keeps running with
no exec(3) after the fork while a cchan from before the fork is
live is not supported. As a defense in depth beyond that rule, the
child never inherits a lock that another thread held, in the locked
state, at the instant of the fork. Neither queue below the channel owns
a thread of its own, so a cchan that a child inherits this way
stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cchan_create_mp (3),
.BR cchan_destroy (3)
